  "rules": {
    "seller_snapshot_boundary_v1": "The Pixel 4a with 5G and Nord N10 pages label renewed phones and provide listing-level prices, aggregate ratings, review counts, specifications, and seller descriptions. The Nord page is internally inconsistent because its top summary shows 11 reviews while its footer shows 12; it also gives generic renewed-condition caveats, a 90-day store warranty, aftermarket accessories, and a carrier-frequency check. The Blackview page advertises ruggedness, IP68, battery, charging, storage, and a one-year warranty. These are frozen seller statements for older catalog items. None is a verified current-unit inspection, current support promise, controlled durability test, population failure rate, or evidence of pleasant use through 2031.",
    "exact_aftermarket_scope_v1": "The DEJI page advertises one replacement-battery kit for iPhone X models A1865, A1901, and A1902, excludes XR and XS, includes tools and instructions, and gives seller quality, capacity, warranty, and review claims. The Dahkoiz page advertises one Galaxy A13 5G case with a tempered-glass protector and port covers, says the case blocks wireless charging unless removed, and gives seller protection and replacement claims. These pages show only that exact-model aftermarket listings existed in the frozen catalog. They do not verify safety, repair success, protection effectiveness, or parts and accessory availability for the three phone listings.",
    "long_use_anecdote_scope_v1": "One author says an iPhone 6s kept since 2016 has a battery needing replacement, working screen and camera, rare freezes, slower behavior, quiet call audio, occasional app crashes, and slow Spotify opening. A second author says an iPhone 7 owned for five years developed a microphone and call-audio problem after a legitimate battery replacement but explicitly asks whether the cause is hardware or software. A third author calls an eight-year iPhone 6 functional but reports several apps becoming incompatible with iOS 12.5. These are individual dated Apple reports. They enumerate possible physical, performance, repair, and app-compatibility endpoints but establish no prevalence, causal relation, brand advantage, or outcome for Pixel, Nord, Blackview, iPhone X, or Galaxy A13 5G.",
    "physical_and_policy_layering_v1": "The screen burn-in page defines permanent display discoloration caused by cumulative non-uniform use and distinguishes it from non-permanent LCD image persistence. The service-life page distinguishes total time in use, manufacturer serviceability or support, technical life, predicted life, and replacement life. The planned-obsolescence page supplies only a generic design and policy concept and a general software end-of-life discussion; it does not establish intent by any captured vendor. Together with the community reports, these sources require separate fields for physical function, replaceable component wear, repair access, manufacturer support, security state, essential-app compatibility, and user task satisfaction. Continued physical operation does not prove current security or app support, and an ended support promise does not by itself identify which hardware component will fail first.",
    "repairability_and_modularity_framework_v1": "The repairability page defines repairability as ease of repair and maintenance and describes documentation, disassembly, spare-parts availability, spare-part pricing, and product-specific factors. The modular-smartphone page says replaceable modules aim to increase lifespan and lower repair costs, while also noting bulk and performance tradeoffs and unclear benefit boundaries. These are generic frameworks, not scores for the captured 5G Pixel, Nord, or Blackview listings and not guarantees of a 2031 outcome. A valid purchase audit must gather exact-model current evidence for parts, manuals, service channels, costs, data-preserving repair, and post-repair checks.",
    "planned_obsolescence_non_accusation_v1": "The planned-obsolescence page defines a general design or policy concept and separately notes that proprietary software normally reaches an end-of-life when maintenance, testing, and support costs exceed revenue. It also distinguishes perceived obsolescence from deliberate planning. This page can motivate an audit of written support and compatibility policy, but it cannot establish intent, artificial shortening, or misconduct by Google, OnePlus, Blackview, Apple, Samsung, DEJI, Dahkoiz, or any seller in this case.",
    "current_evidence_matrix_v1": "For each genuinely current candidate model and region, record purchase channel and exact condition, written security and operating-system support end dates from a current primary source, essential-app requirements, carrier bands and eSIM or physical-SIM needs, battery replacement availability and cost, screen and port repair availability and cost, parts and manuals, data-retention implications, warranty and return window, accessory fit, and a daily-task trial. During the return window test calls, messaging, authentication, camera, maps, payments if needed, connectivity, thermals, battery endurance, charging stability, accessibility, and backup restore. A seller title, aggregate rating, generic repairability framework, or another model's accessory cannot fill a missing cell.",
    "evidence_bounded_2031_choice_v1": "The admissible answer may explain the failure layers, compare the frozen seller snapshots at their exact scope, use the long-use posts as anecdotes, and convert repairability and service-life concepts into a current evidence checklist. It may recommend a current exact model only after all material policy, condition, parts, repair, region, return-window, and task-trial cells are filled with current appropriate evidence. With this frozen corpus alone, the required result is a conditional procurement and maintenance matrix or a deferral, not a universal winner or a 2031 survival promise.",
    "conditional_2031_decision_v1": "The final decision is to defer naming a universal phone from the frozen pool. Treat the three phone pages as old or renewed seller snapshots, keep both aftermarket products at exact-model scope, and use the three Apple posts only as dated failure-sequence anecdotes. Build a current exact-model matrix for support policy, essential apps, parts, manuals, repair routes and costs, condition, region, warranty, return window, accessories, and a daily-task trial. Choose only a candidate that clears every required field, then maintain backups, monitor battery and charging behavior, service replaceable wear items through a qualified route, and define security, app, repair-cost, or task-failure exit triggers. These actions improve decision quality but do not guarantee pleasant use through 2031."
